WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
C++ SBOM work is often more complex than reading a single dependency manifest.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:21.160
A project may use header-only libraries, source-included libraries, static archives, shared objects, package manager recipes, platform packages, generated code, compiler runtime libraries, native build tools, and link-time dependencies.

3
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.060
Some are visible in source control.

4
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.960
Others appear only during the build, packaging, or deployment process.

5
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:37.300
That means SBOM ownership usually has to involve developers, build engineers, release engineers, and security reviewers.

6
00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.220
Direct and transitive dependencies both matter.

7
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:48.880
A team may choose one library, and that library may bring additional components through package metadata, build options, or platform packaging.

8
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.260
Optional features can also change the dependency graph.

9
00:00:52.360 --> 00:01:03.160
A release build for Windows, Linux, and macOS may include different runtime libraries, SDK interfaces, or shared library packaging even when the source repository is the same.

10
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.420
Architecture variants and build profiles can create similar differences.

11
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.940
Vendored code deserves the same attention as package-managed code.

12
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:21.000
Copying source into a repository does not make it first-party design, and generated code is not automatically risk-free just because it appears in the build output.

13
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:31.260
Teams need to understand the generator, the source inputs, the version, and whether the generated files are shipped, linked, or used only during development.

14
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.400
They should also record why the vendored copy exists and how it will be updated.

15
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:44.520
A C++ project may ship one executable while depending on many components that are not obvious from the final file name.

16
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:54.460
Define SBOM scope deliberately: what ships with the release, what shapes the build, what is needed for risk response, and what the organization expects to disclose.

17
00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.800
Clear scope prevents teams from arguing later about whether a missing component was out of scope or simply overlooked.

18
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.580
The scope statement should travel with the SBOM so consumers understand what the file is describing.
